---
import BaseLayout from "../../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import "../../../styles/story.css";
import ReadingModes from "../../../components/ReadingModes.astro";
import Cite from "../../../components/Cite.astro";
import ExplanationList from "../../../components/story/ExplanationList.astro";
import DecisionTimeline from "../../../components/story/DecisionTimeline.astro";
import QuoteCard from "../../../components/story/QuoteCard.astro";
import DataNote from "../../../components/story/DataNote.astro";
import StorySources from "../../../components/StorySources.astro";
import { claim } from "../../../lib/claims.mjs";
import { event, n, povBars, householdBars, chronology, precedents, explanations } from "../../../data/venezuela-oil-2026-11-11-vitol-trader-vance-venezuela-oil-contract.mjs";
const story = "venezuela-oil--2026-11-11-vitol-trader-vance-venezuela-oil-contract";
const short = (id) => claim(story, id).short;
const augustDeal = "/events/venezuela-oil/2026-08-31-100-year-oil-rights-claim/";
const povMax = Math.max(...povBars.map((b) => b.value));
const hhMax = Math.max(...householdBars.map((b) => b.value));
const timeline = chronology.map((e) => ({ date: e.date, title: short(e.c), cites: [{ c: e.c }] }));
---
<BaseLayout title={`${event.title} | The Catch`} current="Events">
  <div slot="locator" class="locator"><a href="/events/">Series</a><a href="/events/venezuela-oil/">Venezuela oil</a><span>November 11, 2026</span></div>
  <div class="content">
    <main class="story" id="story-root">
      <p class="story-kicker" data-layer="fact">Venezuela oil &middot; November 11, 2026 &middot; updated {event.updated}</p>
      <h1 data-layer="fact">{event.title}</h1>
      <p class="story-dek" data-layer="fact">{event.dek}<Cite c="wright-called-addison-jan5" /><Cite c="pov-300k" /><Cite c="no-tender" /></p>
      <ReadingModes />
      <nav class="story-toc" aria-label="In this story" data-layer="fact">
        <details class="toc-disclosure" open><summary><span>In this story</span><span class="toc-current" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary>
        <ol>
          <li><a href="#what-happened">What happened</a></li>
          <li><a href="#the-call">A gasoline trader gets the call</a></li>
          <li><a href="#why-fast">Why a trading house, and why so fast</a></li>
          <li><a href="#the-sides">What each side says</a></li>
          <li><a href="#the-catch">The Catch</a></li>
          <li><a href="#explanations">Every explanation, tested</a></li>
          <li><a href="#coverage">What the coverage got right and wrong</a></li>
          <li><a href="#next">What happens next</a></li>
          <li><a href="#records">The records</a></li>
        </ol>
        </details>
      </nav>

      <section id="what-happened">
        <h2 data-layer="fact">What happened</h2>
        <p data-layer="narrative" data-answer="1">On January 5, two days after U.S. forces captured Nicolás Maduro, Energy Secretary Chris Wright called John Addison, a senior trader at Vitol, which the Energy Department calls the world's largest energy trader, and asked whether Vitol wanted the job of marketing Venezuela's crude.<Cite c="wright-called-addison-jan5" /><Cite c="addison-who" /><Cite c="doe-picked-largest" /> By January 9, Vitol and its rival Trafigura held U.S. Treasury licenses to do it.<Cite c="licenses-by-jan9" /><Cite c="licenses-run-2027" /> There was no tender. Large U.S. government contracts usually go through competitive bidding, to prevent favoritism and get the best price, Reuters notes; the administration skipped it and says it needed the speed to stabilize Venezuela's economy.<Cite c="bidding-norm" /><Cite c="no-tender" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">The job works like this. The traders buy the crude from Venezuela's state oil company, PDVSA, pay for it into an account the United States controls, and resell it to refiners.<Cite c="pdvsa-terms-confidential" /><Cite c="proceeds-settle-account" /><Cite c="first-sale-prices" /> Sources told Reuters the traders bought the first cargoes at $15 a barrel below Brent, the international benchmark price, and Vitol resold some to U.S. Gulf Coast refiners at about ${n.soldUnder} below it.<Cite c="first-sale-prices" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative" data-answer="2">Addison was an unusual person to call. He traded U.S. gasoline, not Latin American crude, and he was not Vitol's regional chief.<Cite c="addison-who" /> He was an early backer of JD Vance: in 2022 he gave $300,000 to the super PAC behind Vance's Senate primary, and ${n.jfc} to Vance's joint fundraising committee.<Cite c="pov-300k" /><Cite c="vance-campaign-direct" /> He has since retired from Vitol and co-chairs Wright's advisory board.<Cite c="addison-retired-board" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative" data-answer="3">The job paid. The two firms have since earned hundreds of millions of dollars marketing the crude, Reuters reports.<Cite c="earned-hundreds-millions" /> They also handled most of it: through September they paid in {n.share}% of the ${n.deposits} billion deposited in the account, by KPMG's count as The New York Times describes it.<Cite c="two-firms-61pct" /><Cite c="proceeds-settle-account" /> That is a share of the account, not of all Venezuela's oil: Chevron and <a href={augustDeal}>NABEP</a>, one of Venezuela's largest private producers, sell at least some of their oil outside it.<Cite c="two-firms-61pct" /><Cite c="nabep-who" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">Their head start outlasted the opening of the trade. On January 29 the Treasury let other established U.S. firms trade the oil, but for the next two weeks, four would-be buyers told Reuters, PDVSA sold only to companies holding individual U.S. licenses, as the two firms did, because it wanted specific U.S. guidance on whom to trade with.<Cite c="gl46-opened" /><Cite c="pdvsa-licensed-only" /><Cite c="licenses-by-jan9" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">Reuters reported on November 11 that Vance had vouched for Addison to Wright.<Cite c="official-vouching-a-year-before" /> From one unnamed source, it also reported that the two men met at Montana's Yellowstone Club on December 27, a week before the raid, and that Vance asked how quickly Venezuelan crude could be sold if the United States gained control of it. Addison and Vance's office deny the meeting.<Cite c="meeting-reported" /><Cite c="meeting-denied" /></p>
      </section>

      <section id="the-call">
        <h2 data-layer="fact">A gasoline trader gets the call</h2>
        <p data-layer="narrative">Addison says he knows why the call came to him: Vance had told Wright he was trustworthy, and that paved the way.<Cite c="addison-vance-vouched" /> A U.S. official confirmed to Reuters that Vance had said so, and told Reuters the conversation happened at least a year before the call.<Cite c="official-vouching-a-year-before" /> The Energy Department says Vance and Wright never discussed the oil sales, and that it picked Vitol because it is the largest trader.<Cite c="doe-picked-largest" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">Addison says he expected a market update when Wright rang.<Cite c="addison-expected-market-update" /> Vitol says he did not have the knowledge to negotiate an international crude contract, and that he brought in Ben Marshall, its Americas chief, and the crude team after the call.<Cite c="vitol-addison-lacked-expertise" /><Cite c="marshall-jan9-quote" /> By Vitol's own account, the man Wright called could not do the deal himself.</p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">On January 9, at a White House meeting with oil and gas executives, Addison told Trump what Vitol and Trafigura were there to do.<Cite c="addison-jan9-quote" /></p>
        <QuoteCard speaker="John Addison, Vitol" when="January 9, 2026" where="White House meeting with oil and gas executives">
          <p>"We're here to ensure that you are going to be able to move all of this oil all around the world at the best price possible so that the influence that you have over the Venezuelans will ensure that you get what you want."<Cite c="addison-jan9-quote" /></p>
        </QuoteCard>
        <p data-layer="narrative">In Addison's telling, the sales were more than a trade: the better the price, the more leverage Trump would have over Venezuela.<Cite c="addison-jan9-quote" /></p>
        <figure class="story-figure" data-layer="fact">
          <figcaption><strong>From the blockade to the account</strong></figcaption>
          <DecisionTimeline entries={timeline} heading="What happened" />
        </figure>

        <h3 data-layer="fact">His political money</h3>
        <p data-layer="narrative">The $300,000 Addison gave in 2022 went to Protect Ohio Values, a super PAC that spent about ${n.povSpent} million on ads, mail, calls and texts, mostly for Vance in his Senate primary. The tech investor Peter Thiel gave $15 million of the ${n.povTotal} million it raised. Addison was its fifth-largest donor, with about {n.addisonPovPct}%.<Cite c="pov-thiel-share" /><Cite c="pov-top-donors" /></p>
        <figure class="story-figure" data-layer="fact">
          <figcaption><strong>The five largest donors to Protect Ohio Values, the super PAC behind Vance's 2022 primary</strong></figcaption>
          <ul class="money-bars" aria-label="Peter Thiel $15 million, Walter Buckley $1.2 million, David Sacks $1 million, Per Aspera Policy $400,000, John Addison $300,000">
            {povBars.map((b) => (
              <li class={b.highlight ? "mb-highlight" : undefined}>
                <span class="mb-label">{b.label}<Cite c={b.c} /></span>
                <span class="mb-track"><span class="mb-bar" style={`width:${Math.max(0.6, (100 * b.value) / povMax)}%`}></span></span>
                <span class="mb-num">{b.shown}</span>
              </li>
            ))}
          </ul>
          <DataNote><p>Federal Election Commission itemized receipts, 2021 to 2022. The super PAC raised ${n.povTotal} million in all.<Cite c="pov-thiel-share" /></p></DataNote>
        </figure>
        <p data-layer="narrative">In 2024 Addison and his wife, Shannon, gave about ${n.household} million to political committees, ${n.trumpNamed} million of it to two committees backing Trump: the super PAC MAGA Inc. and the Trump 47 Committee, a joint fundraising committee.<Cite c="household-7m" /><Cite c="trump-named-5-95m" /> A further $1 million went to Turnout for America, a super PAC turning out swing-state voters for Trump, led by a close friend of Vance's.<Cite c="turnout-for-america" /> In these filings, the money that reached Vance's own campaign was ${n.toCampaign}, in 2022.<Cite c="vance-campaign-direct" /></p>
        <figure class="story-figure" data-layer="fact">
          <figcaption><strong>The Addisons' 2024 gifts to political committees</strong></figcaption>
          <ul class="money-bars" aria-label="2024 gifts by committee">
            {householdBars.map((b) => (
              <li>
                <span class="mb-label">{b.label}<Cite c={b.c} /></span>
                <span class="mb-track"><span class="mb-bar" style={`width:${Math.max(0.6, (100 * b.value) / hhMax)}%`}></span></span>
                <span class="mb-num">{b.shown}</span>
              </li>
            ))}
          </ul>
          <DataNote><p>Federal Election Commission rows at the household's Houston address. The rows carrying the MAGA Inc. and Trump 47 gifts list the employer and title of a different man, Primerica's John A. Addison Jr., who files from Georgia; but they carry the Vitol household's address, the Trump 47 rows his middle initial, and Shannon Addison's gifts beside them, so the gifts are the household's.<Cite c="primerica-field" /><Cite c="household-7m" /></p></DataNote>
        </figure>
      </section>

      <section id="why-fast">
        <h2 data-layer="fact">Why a trading house, and why so fast</h2>
        <p data-layer="narrative">In December Trump ordered a blockade of sanctioned tankers to squeeze Maduro's government.<Cite c="blockade-dec16" /> As storage filled, Bloomberg reported, the blockade forced Venezuela to cut production. On January 28, Secretary of State Marco Rubio told senators that sanctioned oil could not leave "because of our quarantine," his word for the blockade, and that Venezuela needed money at once.<Cite c="storage-under-quarantine" /><Cite c="rubio-short-term" /> By Rubio's account, the oil the traders were brought in to move was stuck because of the U.S. blockade.<Cite c="storage-under-quarantine" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">Trading houses could move that oil quickly. Vitol and Trafigura had been among the most active traders of Venezuelan crude before U.S. sanctions in 2019.<Cite c="traders-past-venezuela" /> The Energy Department said it had engaged "the world’s leading commodity marketers" to carry out the sales, and that U.S.-run sales of Venezuelan oil would continue indefinitely. Rubio called the two traders "a short-term fix."<Cite c="doe-commodity-marketers" /><Cite c="sales-indefinitely" /><Cite c="rubio-short-term" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">On the first sales in January, shipping to the Gulf Coast cost $2.50 to $3.50 a barrel, shipping sources told Reuters, which would leave the traders a margin of $2 to $4 a barrel; one professor puts it at ${n.margin}, and the firms do not disclose profits from individual deals.<Cite c="first-sale-prices" /><Cite c="margin-estimate" /> Wright confirmed the $15 discount on the first $500 million of sales.<Cite c="first-sale-prices" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">The administration's case for the two traders was speed.<Cite c="no-tender" /><Cite c="rubio-short-term" /> Their hold has lasted: they paid in most of the account's deposits through September, by KPMG's count as the Times describes it, and their own licenses, which also cover metals and minerals, run to June 2027, people familiar told Bloomberg in January.<Cite c="two-firms-61pct" /><Cite c="licenses-run-2027" /></p>
      </section>

      <section id="the-sides">
        <h2 data-layer="fact">What each side says</h2>
        <p data-layer="narrative"><strong>The critics.</strong> Rep. Robert Garcia, the top Democrat on the House Oversight Committee, wrote to both firms in January asking about their dealings with the administration; the firms declined to tell Reuters whether they had answered.<Cite c="garcia-advance-notice" /><Cite c="garcia-no-answer" /> Fourteen Senate Democrats objected that the administration was working with two firms with histories of bribery and corruption:<Cite c="senate-dems-records" /> Vitol paid $135 million under a 2020 settlement over bribes to officials at Brazil's state oil company, Petrobras, and Trafigura pleaded guilty in 2024 to bribing Brazilian officials for Petrobras business.<Cite c="vitol-dpa-2020" /><Cite c="trafigura-plea-2024" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative"><strong>The administration.</strong> Asked on CBS in January about allegations that some Trump donors would get preferential treatment, Wright said there was no corruption and no "preferential placement of people," and added, "I'm in the center of this."<Cite c="wright-no-favoritism" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">Both firms say they were given no advance knowledge of the operation.<Cite c="no-advance-knowledge" /> The call itself is not in dispute; why it went to Addison is.<Cite c="wright-called-addison-jan5" /><Cite c="addison-vance-vouched" /><Cite c="doe-picked-largest" /></p>
      </section>

      <section id="the-catch">
        <aside class="the-catch" data-layer="fact">
          <p class="tt-label">The Catch</p>
          <ol>
            <li>
              <h3>The post's pre-raid deal goes further than its one source</h3>
              <p data-layer="narrative">A Spanish-language post on X with more than {n.likes} likes says that a week before the attack the government was already arranging, at a private ski club, to hand Venezuela's oil sales to campaign megadonors without bidding. It says Addison "había donado 300.000 dólares a Vance" (had given $300,000 to Vance) and got the January 5 call on Vance's recommendation.<Cite c="viral-post" /></p>
              <p data-layer="narrative">The post is right that there was no bidding: the administration skipped it, Reuters reports, and says it needed the speed.<Cite c="no-tender" /> It has a basis, too, for the recommendation: a U.S. official told Reuters that Vance told Wright Addison was trustworthy, in a conversation at least a year before the call, and Addison says that paved the way.<Cite c="addison-vance-vouched" /><Cite c="official-vouching-a-year-before" /></p>
              <p data-layer="narrative">On the money, the post is loose rather than wrong. The $300,000 went to Protect Ohio Values, the super PAC that spent about ${n.povSpent} million mostly backing Vance in his primary, not to Vance's campaign; the ${n.jfc} Addison gave Vance's joint fundraising committee passed ${n.toCampaign} to the campaign.<Cite c="pov-300k" /><Cite c="pov-thiel-share" /><Cite c="vance-campaign-direct" /></p>
              <p data-layer="narrative">The deal is where it goes past its source. Reuters' account of a pre-raid meeting comes from one person retelling what Addison told them, and Addison and Vance's office deny it.<Cite c="meeting-reported" /><Cite c="meeting-denied" /> A U.S. official told Reuters that Vance told Wright Addison was trustworthy in a conversation at least a year before the January 5 call.<Cite c="official-vouching-a-year-before" /></p>
              <p data-layer="narrative">Where the post has the government deciding who would get the oil's marketing, Reuters' one source has Vance asking a hypothetical: how fast the oil could be sold if the United States gained control of it.<Cite c="viral-post" /><Cite c="meeting-reported" /></p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>How other oil sales under U.S. license or control were run</h3>
              <p data-layer="narrative">In 2011 the Treasury named Vitol itself in a license to trade oil exported by Libya's rebel council, and let any U.S. person apply to trade it too; in 2022 it named Chevron alone, for its own Venezuelan oil. In occupied Iraq in 2003, the state marketer's first post-war sale went by tender.<Cite c="libya-gl5" /><Cite c="gl41-chevron" /><Cite c="iraq-2003-tender" /> In June, the same Energy Department sought firms to borrow oil from the <a href="/events/fuel-reserves/2026-10-02-g7-releases-100-million-barrels/">Strategic Petroleum Reserve</a> by a public request for proposals, open to competitive bidding, and posted the award, to Vitol.<Cite c="spr-exchange-vitol" /> Venezuela's job was offered by phone, with bidding skipped, Reuters reports.<Cite c="wright-called-addison-jan5" /><Cite c="no-tender" /></p>
              <figure class="story-figure" data-layer="fact">
                <figcaption><strong>How oil has been sold under U.S. control or license</strong></figcaption>
                <table class="data-table precedent-table">
                  <thead><tr><th>Case</th><th>Who sold the oil</th><th>Who could buy and resell it, and how they were picked</th></tr></thead>
                  <tbody>{precedents.map((p) => <tr><td>{p.place}</td><td>{p.seller}</td><td>{p.buyers}{p.c.map((id) => <Cite c={id} />)}</td></tr>)}</tbody>
                </table>
              </figure>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </aside>
      </section>

      <section id="explanations">
        <h2 data-layer="fact">Every explanation, tested</h2>
        <p class="section-intro" data-layer="fact">How Vitol got the job, as each side explains it, with the evidence for and against.</p>
        <ExplanationList entries={explanations} />
      </section>

      <section id="coverage">
        <h2 data-layer="fact">What the coverage got right and wrong</h2>
        <p data-layer="narrative"><strong>Reuters</strong> reported that Addison was "named chairman" of Wright's advisory board; the board's minutes list him as one of two co-chairs.<Cite c="reuters-says-chairman" /> The rest of its report matches the records it rests on: the call the Energy Department confirmed, the vouching an official confirmed, and the meeting, which it attributes to one source and reports Addison and Vance's office denying.<Cite c="wright-called-addison-jan5" /><Cite c="official-vouching-a-year-before" /><Cite c="meeting-reported" /><Cite c="meeting-denied" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative"><strong>10 Things News</strong> summed up the report as a U.S. official confirming that "Vance had recommended Addison to Wright as trustworthy," and wrote that the $300,000 went to Vance's "Senate campaign effort."<Cite c="tenthings-recommended" /> It left out that the official dated the recommendation at least a year before the call, and that the $300,000 went to a super PAC.<Cite c="official-vouching-a-year-before" /><Cite c="pov-300k" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative"><strong>The $6 million and the $7 million.</strong> House Oversight Democrats said in January that Addison had donated $6 million toward Trump's campaign; Reuters says the Addisons gave $7 million to political action committees. Both are right, counting different things: ${n.trumpNamed} million in 2024 to two committees backing Trump, and about ${n.household} million that year to political committees in all.<Cite c="trump-named-5-95m" /><Cite c="household-7m" /></p>
      </section>

      <section id="next">
        <h2 data-layer="fact">What happens next</h2>
        <p data-layer="narrative">Garcia is in line to chair the House Oversight Committee; he says the report "raises even more questions about who knew what and when."<Cite c="garcia-reaction" /> Rep. Mike Levin, a Democrat, wants Wright, Vitol and Addison to testify under oath and the phone records, emails and flight logs produced.<Cite c="levin-testimony" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">What happened to the money in the account is the other open question. In September Senate Democrats wrote that the administration had promised quarterly audits of the account and given KPMG an $84 million contract, yet published none.<Cite c="senate-finance-letter" /> KPMG's report on the account, made public on November 12, counts {n.depositors} depositors and says {n.paidOutPct}% of the money had been paid out by September 30.<Cite c="kpmg-public-report" /> Most of it, ${n.auctions} billion, went to dollar auctions through Venezuela's commercial banks to prop up the bolívar, and the report does not say who ended up with those dollars.<Cite c="beneficiaries-unknown" /></p>
      </section>

      <section id="records">
      <StorySources event={event.slug} />
      </section>
    </main>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .money-bars { list-style: none; padding: 0; margin: 0; font-family: var(--sans); }
  .money-bars li { display: grid; grid-template-columns: minmax(10rem, 1fr) 2fr auto; align-items: center; column-gap: 0.8rem; padding: 0.45rem 0; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--rule); }
  .mb-label { color: var(--ink); font-size: 0.9rem; }
  .mb-track { display: block; height: 0.9rem; background: var(--rule); }
  .mb-bar { display: block; height: 100%; background: var(--slate); }
  .mb-highlight .mb-bar { background: var(--blue); }
  .mb-highlight .mb-label, .mb-highlight .mb-num { font-weight: 600; }
  .mb-num { font-size: 0.85rem; color: var(--ink); white-space: nowrap; text-align: right; min-width: 6rem; }
  .story-figure .data-table td, .story-figure .data-table th { word-break: normal; overflow-wrap: normal; hyphens: none; }
  @media (max-width: 640px) {
    .money-bars li { grid-template-columns: 1fr auto; row-gap: 0.25rem; }
    .money-bars .mb-track { grid-column: 1 / -1; grid-row: 2; }
  }
</style>
